{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"","type":"thinking"},{"text":"Confirmed safe on the parsing side, which matters because `#` was the obvious landmine: comments are **full-line only** (`eval.rs:218` trims then checks `starts_with('#')`), so a `#commit/path` mid-line survives. And the split points are all safe for URL values — `split_once('=')` takes the *first* `=` (so a `?rev=` query is fine), and the `:`-type split runs on the **key** only (`--foo:@@`), never the value, so `https://`'s colon is untouched. So the value can be a whole URL with a fragment and a query and nothing eats it. One real constraint: you can't trail a comment after such a line, but you can't anywhere today.\n\n## Mandatory hash — and it's forced to be a *commit*, not a tree\n\nYou're right that mandatory-hash is what makes it content-addressed-with-provenance rather than a mutable name. But there's a second, independent reason it lands on your `#commit/path` shape specifically: **you can't fetch a bare tree/blob oid from GitHub.** GitHub only serves `allowReachableSHA1InWant` for *commits* — `git fetch --depth 1 <url> <sha>` works for a commit, not for a subtree hash. So the fetchable granularity *is* the commit, and the path selects within it. Your instinct wasn't just ergonomic; it's the only thing the transport allows. Commit-first in the fragment is also the right order: commit is fixed-width hex, so split-on-first-`/` cleanly separates it from the variable path.\n\n## What lands in the arg tree\n\nKeep resolution at eval time reducing to a **plain tree/blob oid** — the commit and URL are *fetch coordinates*, not part of the key. Fetch `--depth 1 <url> <commit>`, peel to tree, descend `<path>`, and the resulting oid is the arg entry, byte-for-byte as if it came from a local `:@=`. That's what makes two consumers pinning the same caos rev share the whole deepened std subgraph by hash, and it's what keeps the URL out of every cache key. Provenance (which URL/commit it came from) can ride the trace, not the identity.\n\n## Naming — I'd go `:@@=`, and here's the clincher\n\nThe reason it *can't* just be smarter `:@=` (sniff the value for a URL) is the codebase's stated rule: types are chosen by the operator, never by sniffing the value, \"so a value is never misread and may contain anything.\" A URL-shaped path in a private repo would be misread. So it genuinely needs its own type — which is what you're circling.\n\nGiven that, `:@@=` is the one I'd pick, because the mental model becomes a clean pair:\n\n- `:@=<path>` — a path in the **ambient** repo (cwd/worktree).\n- `:@@=<url>#<commit>/<path>` — the same path, but in **that** repo at that commit.\n\nThe doubled `@` reads as \"a git path, but over there,\" and the \"path into the repo\" semantics — the part `:git-repo=` failed to convey — come *for free* from the `@` lineage, since a `:@=` value already **is** a path. That's the elegant bit: you're not inventing \"repo+path,\" you're saying \"`@`, elsewhere.\" `:from=`/`:git=` are more readable to a newcomer but lose that symmetry; your call on which audience wins.\n\nTwo grammar details I'd pin:\n- Canonical scheme `git+https://…` (and `git+ssh://` for private), with `github:owner/repo#commit/path` as client-expanded sugar. The `git+` prefix is what lets **image position** — where there's no `:type=`, only a bare token — dispatch a locator by shape, alongside the existing `docker://`/hash/path sniffing. (Args get it by explicit tag; image position by shape. That asymmetry already exists, so it's consistent.)\n- Validate the fragment's commit is a **full-length hex sha**, and error loudly on a branch/tag name — that rejection is precisely what enforces \"must include a hash.\"\n\n## The bonus this buys you (and a boundary it exposes)\n\nThis primitive doesn't just replace last turn's nix-expander — it dissolves most of it. A **non-nix consumer needs no expander at all**: they hand-write `run git+https://…/caos#<rev>/std/deep-deps -- …` and pin caos directly. Zero nix in core, zero nix in the consumer.\n\nWhich sharpens where the flake angle actually goes: a *worker can parse `flake.lock` to extract caos's rev (pure), but a worker can't fetch (network is a client capability)*, and the eval grammar's `$VAR` is an object-reference, not a string you can splice into a URL fragment. So \"auto-pin from `flake.lock`\" can't be runtime eval magic without dragging nix-awareness into the client. The honest home for it is **lockfile codegen**: a dev/build hook that, when you `nix flake update`, writes the resolved `git+https://…#<rev>` locator into a tracked generated file (or the `.caos-expr` line). That keeps the pin single-sourced from `flake.lock`, keeps core and the evaluator entirely nix-free, and is exactly the \"generated, not committed-by-hand, not resolved-at-runtime\" seam this codebase already likes.\n\nNet grammar I'd propose:\n\n```\n--name:@@=git+https://github.com/company/repo#<40-hex-commit>/path/into/repo   # arg\nrun       git+https://github.com/company/repo#<40-hex-commit>/std/deep-deps    # image position\n```\n\nresolving client-side, at eval time, to a bare tree/blob oid.\n\nWant me to trace exactly where this slots in — the arg-type parse (`lib.rs` `split_once(':')` around 2206 + the `eval.rs:395` type match) and the image-position sniff in `resolve_expr_image`/`resolve_run_image` — and where the fetch-and-verify helper would live?","type":"text"}],"results":[{"content":[{"text":"crates/caos/src/eval.rs:12://! ## Grammar\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:14://! One `.caos-expr` is a sequence of lines. Blank lines and `#` comments are\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:21://! curry $NAME -- --worker1:@=path         # the value (a run/curry, or a bare $NAME)\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:29://! ## Argument resolution\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:132:        .split('/')\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:184:/// - **The directive stops keying its own input.** Editing a comment in\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:218:        let line = raw.trim();\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:219:        if line.is_empty() || line.starts_with('#') {\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:253:    match rest.split_whitespace().next() {\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:269:        if var.is_empty() || var.split_whitespace().count() != 1 {\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:290:    let tokens: Vec<&str> = cmd.split_whitespace().collect();\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:348:    if tok.starts_with(crate::DOCKER_SCHEME) {\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:393:            .split_once('=')\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:395:        let (name, is_path) = match key.split_once(':') {\ncrates/caos/src/eval.rs:482:        .split('/')","type":"text"}],"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gw34LLjEdbk2mrE6VdfnRQ","type":"tool_result"}],"v":1}